      "text": "smith-rebound re-derives gate_classification from fresh data even when the orchestrator already hands it the authoritative gate inline in the dispatch prompt -- on the 2026-09-01 run it re-fetched VIX and all 4 Asia indices to 'independently check' a value it was explicitly told to treat as authoritative, which is most of why it ran 150s against its own <90s budget (it stayed within its 8-call cap, but the extra fetch pass ate the time budget). Its own agent file's rule D.2 ('may ratchet severity UP on fresher data but forbids downgrading without re-checking all v2 terms') was written to prevent silently trusting a stale gate after G42 -- but it does not require re-deriving the SAME-run gate the orchestrator just computed seconds earlier, only a materially older one. Fixed 2026-09-01: smith-rebound.md now distinguishes 'the orchestrator's this-run gate, stated in the dispatch prompt' (trust it, cite it) from 'a gate carried over from a prior run's stale state' (the actual G42 case rule D.2 exists for) -- only the second case triggers independent re-verification. Generalise: a standing-order rule written to fix one incident (trusting STALE data) can accidentally also block trusting FRESH data from the same run if the rule doesn't distinguish the two -- check which one a re-verification instruction is actually guarding against before applying it broadly.",
